d C=\\{1,2,{\\color{red}4}\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that *every* element of $A$ is also an element of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}}\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{3}}\\in B\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is a subset of $B,$ and we write $A \\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $A$ and $B$ as shown below. Notice that $A$ is contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/91/911dbbe9a638810f3a245fdcd004e10aee4dbd49fd8a99741c1bdd5c48cc2319.png)\n\nOn the other hand, not every element of $C$ is an element of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n1 \\in B, \\qquad 2\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{red}4}\\notin B\n$$\n\nSince $4 \\notin B,$ we have that $C$ is *not* a subset of $B,$ and we write $C \\not\\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $B$ and $C$ as follows. Notice that $C$ is *not* contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/45/454c28bacb8d5117bb11f76988b70344b4521d260adf2b690a11e7dfd2964385.png)\n\nAnother way of thinking about the statement $C \\not\\subseteq B$ is that $C$ contains *at least one* element that is not an element of $B.$ In this case, that element is $4.$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186056044 1064 "Given two sets $A$ and $B,$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B,$ we say that $A$ is a **subset** of $B,$ and we write \n\n$$\nA \\subseteq B.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ {\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{blue}{3}} \\}, \\qquad B = \\{ 1,2,3\\}, \\qquad C=\\{1,2,{\\color{red}4}\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that *every* element of $A$ is also an element of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}}\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{3}}\\in B\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is a subset of $B,$ and we write $A \\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $A$ and $B$ as shown below. Notice that $A$ is contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/91/911dbbe9a638810f3a245fdcd004e10aee4dbd49fd8a99741c1bdd5c48cc2319.png)\n\nOn the other hand, not every element of $C$ is an element of $B{:}$ \n\n$$\n1 \\in B, \\qquad 2\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{red}4}\\notin B\n$$\n\nSince $4 \\notin B,$ we have that $C$ is *not* a subset of $B,$ and we write $C \\not\\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $B$ and $C$ as follows. Notice that $C$ is *not* contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/45/454c28bacb8d5117bb11f76988b70344b4521d260adf2b690a11e7dfd2964385.png)\n\nAnother way of thinking about the statement $C \\not\\subseteq B$ is that $C$ contains *at least one* element that is not an element of $B.$ In this case, that element is $4.$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186056056 1064 "Recall that the natural numbers, integers, rational numbers, and real and complex numbers can be arranged in the following hierarchical diagram.\n\n![](images/66/66260e4c239137cd30453d9335256fd7d84e93a197612d670c19d97e417bd2f0.png)\n\nIn other words, $\\mathbb{N}$ is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Z},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Q},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{R},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{C}.$ We can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Z} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R} \\subseteq \\mathbb{C}\n$$\n\nSince none of these sets are equivalent, we can also write\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z} \\subset \\mathbb{Q} \\subset \\mathbb{R} \\subset \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nSome restricted or extended special sets are subsets of others. For example:\n\n- $\\mathbb N$ is the set of natural numbers, and $\\mathbb N_0$ is the set of natural numbers *and* zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\mathbb N\\subset \\mathbb N_0.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Z$ is the set of integers, $\\mathbb Z^+$ is the set of positive integers (equivalent to $\\mathbb N$), and $\\mathbb Z^+_0$ is the set of positive integers and zero (equivalent to $\\mathbb N_0$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z^+\\subset  \\mathbb Z_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Q^+$ is the set of positive rational numbers, $\\mathbb R^+$ is the set of positive real numbers, and $\\mathbb R$ is the set of real numbers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Q^+\\subset  \\mathbb R^+ \\subset \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nHowever, note that, for example, $\\mathbb Q \\not\\subset  \\mathbb R^+,$ since $0\\in\\mathbb Q$ yet $0\\notin \\mathbb R^+.$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186056056 1064 "Recall that the natural numbers, integers, rational numbers, and real and complex numbers can be arranged in the following hierarchical diagram.\n\n![](images/66/66260e4c239137cd30453d9335256fd7d84e93a197612d670c19d97e417bd2f0.png)\n\nIn other words, $\\mathbb{N}$ is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Z},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Q},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{R},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{C}.$ We can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Z} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R} \\subseteq \\mathbb{C}\n$$\n\nSince none of these sets are equivalent, we can also write\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z} \\subset \\mathbb{Q} \\subset \\mathbb{R} \\subset \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nSome restricted or extended special sets are subsets of others. For example:\n\n- $\\mathbb N$ is the set of natural numbers, and $\\mathbb N_0$ is the set of natural numbers *and* zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\mathbb N\\subset \\mathbb N_0.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Z$ is the set of integers, $\\mathbb Z^+$ is the set of positive integers (equivalent to $\\mathbb N$), and $\\mathbb Z^+_0$ is the set of positive integers and zero (equivalent to $\\mathbb N_0$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z^+\\subset  \\mathbb Z_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Q^+$ is the set of positive rational numbers, $\\mathbb R^+$ is the set of positive real numbers, and $\\mathbb R$ is the set of real numbers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Q^+\\subset  \\mathbb R^+ \\subset \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nHowever, note that, for example, $\\mathbb Q \\not\\subset  \\mathbb R^+,$ since $0\\in\\mathbb Q$ yet $0\\notin \\mathbb R^+.$\n\nFrom these basic inclusions, we can now deduce some subset relationships for other special numerical sets. For example, recall that [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] represents the set of all non-negative integers. \n\n* Obviously, [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] is a proper subset of [math]\\mathbb{Z}[/math] since all the elements of [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] are integers but there are some integers, say, [math]-1,[/math] that does not belong to [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.[/math] So, [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb{Z}.[/math]\n\n* On the other hand, [math]\\mathbb{N}[/math] is a proper subset of [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] since all natural number lie in [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] but [math]0 \\notin \\mathbb{N}.[/math] So, [math]\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.[/math]\n\nTherefore, we have [math]\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z}_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb{Z}.[/math]" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186056075 1064 "You might wonder why we need to define a universal set. Can't we just let the universal set be the set of everything?\n\nThe answer is no. If we don't specify a well-defined universal set, we encounter a nonsensical situation called **Russell's paradox.**\n\nFirst, suppose there exists a set $\\Omega$ that contains absolutely everything, including every mathematical object, every thought, every book, every person, animal, cell, and star in the universe, past, present, or future. \n\nRussell's paradox originates from the following idea:\n\n*Define $S$ as the set that contains all sets that are not elements of themselves.* \n\n$$\nS = \\{A\\in \\Omega\\,:\\, A\\notin A\\}\n$$\n\nMost sets you can think of are elements of $S.$ For example, the set $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ is in $S$ because $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}\\notin \\{ 1,2,3 \\}.$ \n\nIt takes a bit of thought to find sets that *aren't* members of $S.$ Such sets do exist, although typical examples are somewhat abstract: \n\n- The set containing all possible sets.\n\n- The set containing all possible thoughts.\n\n- The set $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX = \\Big\\{\\underbrace{\\big\\{ \\left\\{\\{\\ldots \\}\\right\\}\\big\\}}_{X}\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nHowever, what about $S$ itself? Is $S$ an element of $S?$ There are only two possibilities:\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction because we cannot have $S\\in S$ *and* $S\\notin S.$\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we must have that $S\\in S.$ But this leads to the same contradiction as before!\n\nThe only way to resolve this is to conclude that $S\\notin \\Omega,$ but this is also a contradiction since $\\Omega$ contains absolutely everything!  So, there is no consistent answer to the question of whether $S$ itself is an element of $S.$ This is Russell's paradox, and when it was discovered, it shook the foundations of mathematics to its very core.\n\nLater, it was realized that if a set is defined using a predicate, we should ensure that all sets involved are subsets of some larger set $U$ (i.e., a universal set) where we can always determine which elements are members of $U$ and which are not, at least in principle.\n\nTo see how this resolves Russell's paradox, suppose $U$ is a universal set. Let's refine our definition of $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{A\\subseteq U\\,:\\,A\\notin A\\}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume $S$ is a set, so $S\\subseteq U$ for some well-defined $U.$ Once again, we'll ask the question of whether $S\\in S{:}$\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction.\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\in S.$ But this is also a contradiction.\n\nThus, to resolve the paradox, we're left with no choice but to conclude that $S\\not\\subseteq U.$ This indicates that something is fundamentally wrong with our definition of $S,$ and no such set can exist because if it were a set, it would belong to a universal set." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186056075 1064 "You might wonder why we need to define a universal set. Can't we just let the universal set be the set of everything?\n\nThe answer is no. If we don't specify a well-defined universal set, we encounter a nonsensical situation called **Russell's paradox.**\n\nFirst, suppose there exists a set $\\Omega$ that contains absolutely everything, including every mathematical object, every thought, every book, every person, animal, cell, and star in the universe, past, present, or future. \n\nRussell's paradox originates from the following idea:\n\n> *Define $S$ as the set that contains all sets that are not elements of themselves.*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> S = \\{A\\in \\Omega\\,:\\, A\\notin A\\}\n> $$\n\nMost sets you can think of are elements of $S.$ For example, the set $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ is in $S$ because $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}\\notin \\{ 1,2,3 \\}.$ \n\nIt takes a bit of thought to find sets that *aren't* members of $S.$ Such sets do exist, although typical examples are somewhat abstract: \n\n- The set containing all possible sets.\n\n- The set containing all possible thoughts.\n\n- The set $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX = \\Big\\{\\underbrace{\\big\\{ \\left\\{\\{\\ldots \\}\\right\\}\\big\\}}_{X}\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nHowever, what about $S$ itself? Is $S$ an element of $S?$ There are only two possibilities:\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction because we cannot have $S\\in S$ *and* $S\\notin S.$\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we must have that $S\\in S.$ But this leads to the same contradiction as before!\n\nThe only way to resolve this is to conclude that $S\\notin \\Omega,$ but this is also a contradiction since $\\Omega$ contains absolutely everything!  So, there is no consistent answer to the question of whether $S$ itself is an element of $S.$ This is Russell's paradox, and when it was discovered, it shook the foundations of mathematics to its very core.\n\nThe issue, unknown to mathematicians until later, is that the object [math]S[/math] is not a set. If it were, Russell's paradox would not occur since we should always be able to determine whether an object is a member of a set or not.\n\nLater, it was realized that if a set is defined using a predicate, we should ensure that all sets involved are subsets of some larger set $U$ (i.e., a universal set) where we can always determine which elements are members of $U$ and which are not, at least in principle.\n\nTo see how this resolves Russell's paradox, suppose $U$ is a universal set. Let's refine our definition of $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{A\\subseteq U\\,:\\,A\\notin A\\}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume $S$ is a set, so $S\\subseteq U$ for some well-defined $U.$ Once again, we'll ask the question of whether $S\\in S{:}$\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction.\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\in S.$ But this is also a contradiction.\n\nThus, to resolve the paradox, we're left with no choice but to conclude that $S\\not\\subseteq U.$ This indicates that something is fundamentally wrong with our definition of $S,$ and no such set can exist because if it were a set, it would belong to a universal set. \n\n* If [math]S\\notin S,[/math] then by the definition of [math]S[/math] and applying De Morgan's law for conjunctions, we have\n\\begin{align*}\nS\\notin S \\Leftrightarrow \\lnot (S\\subseteq U\\land  S\\notin S)  \\equiv (S\\not\\subseteq U) \\lor (S\\in S).\n\\end{align*}" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186068714 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561036 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186068714 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561054 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186068715 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561058 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186075711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560785 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186075711 1078 #edb/ref 17592186560785 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186075712 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560423 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186075712 1078 #edb/ref 17592186560423 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078187 1071 "What is the index of the term $42$ in the following arithmetic sequence?\n\n$$\n-10, \\: -6, \\: -2, \\: \\ldots\n$$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078187 1071 "What is the index of the term $42$ in the following arithmetic sequence?\n$-10, \\: -6, \\: -2, \\: \\ldots$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186078187 1074 "Since $a_1=-10$ and\n\n$$\nd=-6-(-10)=4\n$$\n\nwe can construct the formula for the $n$th term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&=-10 + (n - 1)(4) \\\\\n&=-10 + 4n - 4 \\\\\n&= 4n - 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=42$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 4n - 14 \\\\\n42 &= 4n - 14 \\\\\n56 &= 4n \\\\\n14 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $42$ is the $14$th term of the sequence." 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186078187 1074 "Since $a_1=-10$ and \n\n$$\nd=-6-(-10)=4,\n$$\n\nwe can construct the formula for the $n$th term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-10+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =-10+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=42,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =4n-14 \\\\ 42 & =4n-14 \\\\ 56 & =4n \\\\ 14 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $42$ is the $14$th term of the sequence." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078188 1071 "Given the arithmetic sequence\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + 12, \\quad a_1 = 3,\\quad n\\geq 1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the index of the term $255?$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078188 1071 "Given the arithmetic sequence\n\n$a_{n+1} = a_n + 12, \\quad a_1 = 3,\\quad n\\geq 1$,\nwhat is the index of the term $255$?" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186078188 1074 "We will convert the recursive formula to an explicit formula and then solve for the value of $n$ such that $a_n=255$.\n\nThe recursive rule for an arithmetic sequence takes the form\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n +{\\color{black}d}\n$$\n\nIn the given formula, we have\n\n$$\nd={\\color{black}12}\n$$\n\nWe are also told that\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{black}3}\n$$\n\nso we can write the explicit formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&= 3 + (n - 1)(12) \\\\\n&= 3 + 12n - 12 \\\\\n&= 12n - 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ such that $a_n=255$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 12n - 9 \\\\\n255 &= 12n - 9 \\\\\n264 &= 12n \\\\\n\\frac{264}{12} &= n \\\\\n22 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $255$ is the $22$nd term of the sequence." 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186078188 1074 "We will convert the recursive formula to an explicit formula and then solve for the value of $n$ such that $a_n=255.$\n\nThe recursive rule for an arithmetic sequence takes the form\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n +{\\color{black}d}.\n$$\n\nIn the given formula, we have $d={\\color{black}12}.$ We are also told that $a_1={\\color{black}3},$ so we can write the explicit formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =3+(n-1)(12) \\\\  & =3+12n-12 \\\\  & =12n-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ such that $a_n=255\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =12n-9 \\\\ 255 & =12n-9 \\\\ 264 & =12n \\\\ \\frac{264}{12} & =n \\\\ 22 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $255$ is the $22$nd term of the sequence." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078189 1071 "Given an arithmetic sequence that has $a_4=5$ and common difference $d=3$, what is the index of the term $95$?" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186078189 1071 "Given an arithmetic sequence that has $a_4=5$ and common difference $d=3$, what is the index of the term $95?$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078189 1074 "Since $a_4=5$ is $3$ jumps after $a_1$, we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{4} &= a_{1} + 3d \\\\\n5 &= a_{1} + 3(3) \\\\\n5 &= a_{1} + 9 \\\\\n- 4 &= a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&=-4 + (n - 1)(3) \\\\\n&=-4 + 3n - 3 \\\\\n&= 3n - 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=95$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 3n - 7 \\\\\n95 &= 3n - 7 \\\\\n102 &= 3n \\\\\n\\frac{102}{3} &= n \\\\\n34 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $95$ is the $34$th term of the sequence." 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186078189 1074 "Since $a_4=5$ is $3$ jumps after $a_1,$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+3d \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}+3(3) \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}+9 \\\\ -4 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-4+(n-1)(3) \\\\  & =-4+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=95,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3n-7 \\\\ 95 & =3n-7 \\\\ 102 & =3n \\\\ \\frac{102}{3} & =n \\\\ 34 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $95$ is the $34$th term of the sequence." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078191 1071 "Given an arithmetic sequence that has $a_2=12$ and $a_{4} = 10$, what is the index of the term $-12$?" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186078191 1071 "Given an arithmetic sequence that has $a_2=12$ and $a_{4} = 10$, what is the index of the term $-12?$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078191 1074 "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term $a_1$.\n\nSince we are given $a_2=12$ and $a_4=10$, we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{4} - a_{2}}{4 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10 - 12}{4 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2}{2} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $a_2=12$ is $1$ jump after $a_1$, we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= a_{1} + d \\\\\n12 &= a_{1} - 1 \\\\\n13 &= a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&= 13 + (n - 1)(-1) \\\\\n&= 14 - n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=-12$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 14 - n \\\\\n- 12 &= 14 - n \\\\\n- 26 &= -n \\\\\n26 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-12$ is the $26$th term of the sequence." 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186078191 1074 "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term $a_1.$\n\nSince we are given $a_2=12$ and $a_4=10,$ we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{4}-{a}_{2}}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-12}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $a_2=12$ is $1$ jump after $a_1,$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+d \\\\ 12 & ={a}_{1}-1 \\\\ 13 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =13+(n-1)(-1) \\\\  & =14-n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=-12,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =14-n \\\\ -12 & =14-n \\\\ -26 & =-n \\\\ 26 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-12$ is the $26$th term of the sequence." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078468 1038 #edb/ref 17592186560979 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078468 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561021 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078468 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561050 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186560981 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078470 1038 #edb/ref 17592186560983 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078470 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561051 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078471 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561039 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078471 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561052 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078687 1050 900.0 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078780 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078780 1071 "Given the arithmetic sequence\n$a_{n + 1} = a_{n} - 6, a_{1} = 2, n \\ge 1$,\nthe index of the term $-124$ is $n =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186078780 1071 "Given the arithmetic sequence \n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}-6,\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nthe index of the term $-124$ is $n=${{field-1}}." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078780 1074 "We will convert the recursive formula to an explicit formula and then solve for the value of $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=-124.$\n\nThe recursive rule for an arithmetic sequence takes the form\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+d.\n$$\n\nIn the given formula, we have $d=-6.$ We are also told that ${a}_{1}=2,$ so we can write the explicit formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =2+(n-1)(-6) \\\\  & =2-6n+6 \\\\  & =-6n+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=-124:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-6n+8 \\\\ -124 & =-6n+8 \\\\ -132 & =-6n \\\\ \\frac{-132}{-6} & =n \\\\ 22 & =n \\\\ n & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-124$ is the $22$nd term of the sequence." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078781 1071 "If an arithmetic sequence has $a_{6} = 12$ and $a_{9} = 24$, find the value of $n$ such that $a_{n} = 132$.$n =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186078781 1071 "If an arithmetic sequence has ${a}_{6}=12$ and ${a}_{9}=24$, find the value of $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=132.$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186078781 1074 "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term ${a}_{1}.$\n\nSince we are given ${a}_{6}=12$ and ${a}_{9}=24,$ we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{9}-{a}_{6}}{9-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-12}{9-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{6}=12$ is $5$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}+5d \\\\ 12 & ={a}_{1}+5(4) \\\\ 12 & ={a}_{1}+20 \\\\ -8 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-8+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =-8+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=132,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =4n-12 \\\\ 132 & =4n-12 \\\\ 144 & =4n \\\\ n & =\\frac{144}{4} \\\\ n & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $132$ is the $36$th term of the sequence." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186079730 1064 "In addition to using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto compute the terms of an arithmetic sequence, we can also use it to compute the **index** of a particular term. The index of a term is simply the value of $n$ corresponding to that term.\n\nTo illustrate, suppose we're given the following arithmetic sequence:\n\n$$\n1, \\: 4, \\: 7, \\: 10, \\ \\ldots, \\: 28, \\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nIf the first term is $1$, the second term is $4$, the third term is $7$, and so on, then what term gives the value $28$? In other words, what value of $n$ gives $a_n=28$?\n\nSince $a_1=1$ and $d=3$, we can construct the formula for the $n$th term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&= 1 + (n - 1)(3) \\\\\n&= 1 + 3n - 3 \\\\\n&= 3n - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=28$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 3n - 2 \\\\\n28 &= 3n - 2 \\\\\n30 &= 3n \\\\\n10 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $28$ is the $10$th term of the sequence." 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186079730 1064 "In addition to using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto compute the terms of an arithmetic sequence, we can also use it to compute the **index** of a particular term. The index of a term is simply the value of $n$ corresponding to that term.\n\nTo illustrate, suppose we're given the following arithmetic sequence:\n\n$$\n1, \\: 4, \\: 7, \\: 10, \\ \\ldots, \\: 28, \\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nIf the first term is $1,$ the second term is $4,$ the third term is $7,$ and so on, then what term gives the value $28?$ In other words, what value of $n$ gives $a_n=28?$\n\nSince $a_1=1$ and $d=3,$ we can construct the formula for the $n$th term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =1+(n-1)(3) \\\\  & =1+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=28,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3n-2 \\\\ 28 & =3n-2 \\\\ 30 & =3n \\\\ 10 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $28$ is the $10$th term of the sequence." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186083186 1038 #edb/ref 17592186560993 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186083186 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561017 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186083186 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561024 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186083186 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561065 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186083187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186560972 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186083187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561003 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186083187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561032 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186083187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561041 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186083188 1038 #edb/ref 17592186560988 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186083188 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561014 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186083188 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561025 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186083188 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561028 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186087752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186560997 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186087752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561057 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186087934 1050 420.0 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186088930 1064 "A **dilation** of scale factor $k$ with the center at the origin is a linear transformation that multiplies the standard basis vectors by $k.$ It is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor example, let \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n be the matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ This transformation is a dilation of scale factor $2.$\n\nTo visualize the dilation $\\mathbf{T},$ consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square $\\mathrm{R}$ spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/6e/6e38185922e9cad6d3961e7c5e755eabf4c52eafd1cc81a1e81a383b4df5ae82.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =Ti=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=2i, \\\\ {j}^{'} & =Tj=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ multiplies vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ by $2.$ The resulting vectors span a larger region, shown below.\n\n![](images/cc/cc6677c641a2a713e0324db90edfdd86a5ca1c096c0ffd70f1216119f36da645.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186088930 1064 "A **dilation** of scale factor $k$ with the center at the origin is a linear transformation that multiplies the standard basis vectors by $k.$ It is represented by the matrix\n![](images/0b/0bad96759d0f794111219092aebafa8242d11ab8adf1bd4dbec31d28f745c766.svg)\n\nFor example, let ![](images/f6/f6c5df003fc9570c1a7ee6111f778f79f27bdffd61d717ee3a5ebf6e53c9718e.svg) be the matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ This transformation is a dilation of scale factor $2.$\n\nTo visualize the dilation $\\mathbf{T},$ consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square $\\mathrm{R}$ spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/6e/6e38185922e9cad6d3961e7c5e755eabf4c52eafd1cc81a1e81a383b4df5ae82.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ti=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2i, \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Tj=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ multiplies vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ by $2.$ The resulting vectors span a larger region, shown below.\n\n![](images/cc/cc6677c641a2a713e0324db90edfdd86a5ca1c096c0ffd70f1216119f36da645.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186088931 1064 "A **reflection in the $x$-axis** is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo visualize a reflection in the $x$-axis, consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square $\\mathrm{R}$ spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/b9/b93bb91dae85a3e3b40a8d0ade3d4f420064aaa6926431fa7f664c977bb8fd59.png)\n\nSimilarly, a **reflection in the $y$-axis** is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=-i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/49/4993d8586a84e4a40a54edddd8011cd3d516264bfffc7e3c33d27a10edf1e7d7.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186088931 1064 "A **reflection in the $x$-axis** is represented by the matrix\n![](images/96/96d1e42ccb0e19c6a7fe605af0cbd17ded39967798c9804bf7514adc845c44b8.svg)\n\nTo visualize a reflection in the $x$-axis, consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square $\\mathrm{R}$ spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/b9/b93bb91dae85a3e3b40a8d0ade3d4f420064aaa6926431fa7f664c977bb8fd59.png)\n\nSimilarly, a **reflection in the $y$-axis** is represented by the matrix\n![](images/40/40e77688a74462b8d0d29bca87db283c4ccb960fad6f1ac7cbac716a73f7b8e0.svg)\n\nAgain, we can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/49/4993d8586a84e4a40a54edddd8011cd3d516264bfffc7e3c33d27a10edf1e7d7.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186088956 1064 "A **reflection in line $y=x$** is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=j \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/0e/0e37a258ea833a2d490fc34a34af40a5c7a18f7589f107c6d0ed631b331c8f2a.png)\n\nSimilarly, a **reflection in line $y=-x$** is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-j \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/4a/4a83673a409fd2e5d33b0b5f743f57af08cfb27c38e312a636179102c2172b04.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186088956 1064 "A **reflection in line $y=x$** is represented by the matrix\n![](images/d6/d6c5ff7f9c96510f6243059a023a32026a3ea0dd181a2b07bdf82147de887361.svg)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/0e/0e37a258ea833a2d490fc34a34af40a5c7a18f7589f107c6d0ed631b331c8f2a.png)\n\nSimilarly, a **reflection in line $y=-x$** is represented by the matrix\n![](images/f4/f450c3581b23abb715ba02d7459b043f6414cbd06e50bafdafbdb011e4160625.svg)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/4a/4a83673a409fd2e5d33b0b5f743f57af08cfb27c38e312a636179102c2172b04.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186110644 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561000 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186110645 1038 #edb/ref 17592186560994 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186110645 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561059 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186115354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561016 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186115354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561061 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186115355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561009 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186115356 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561029 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186115482 1050 420.0 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186123896 1078 #edb/ref 17592186123901 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186124584 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561042 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186124634 1038 #edb/ref 17592186560996 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186124635 1038 #edb/ref 17592186560991 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186124635 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561001 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186124641 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561031 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186124642 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561043 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186124659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186560980 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186124660 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561015 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186125216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186125216 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(0.5,0.5)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186125216 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n$A = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: \\frac{x^{2}}{3} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2} \\le 1}, ∂A = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: \\frac{x^{2}}{3} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2} = 1}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The point $Q(-1, \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n2. The point $P(0.5, 0.5)$ belongs to $A$\n3. The point $Q(-1, \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186125216 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0.5,0.5)$ and $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0.5,0.5)$  satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186127441 1078 #edb/ref 17592186127444 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186127549 1078 #edb/ref 17592186127550 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186127807 1078 #edb/ref 17592186127812 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186127885 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127886 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186127885 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127887 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186127885 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127888 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186127885 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127889 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186127885 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127890 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186127885 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560429 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186127885 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560530 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186127885 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560531 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186127885 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560538 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186127885 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560658 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186127885 1078 #edb/ref 17592186560538 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186128885 1071 "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\n- ![](images/52/5220f02f62545b84430a05787db178ae2192d7f8d4ac50c09c481ceca1c77aa1.png)\n\n- ![](images/21/2141e569faba4a24d491d7c9e6356cffc6d