 horizontally by $\\color{blue}1$ unit *right*:\n\n![](images/d4/d4778ef277b11ab33eb01a650ad07c4fd2e3902f303dd2fb15ed81a4b54649a0.png)\n\nThe graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x+h}\n$$\n\nis a horizontal translation of the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nby $h$ units *left*.\n\nFor example, to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x{\\color{red} \\, + \\, 3}}\n$$\n\nwe translate the original curve by $\\color{red}3$ units *left*:\n\n![](images/87/87bbb7c00e10db814c9fc67786940cf5891d96496a2e85ed7ee2023bb1af8e3d.png)\n\n**Watch out!** Horizontal translations are always counter-intuitive. The translation\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x-h}\n$$\n\nis to the right and the translation\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x+h}\n$$\n\nis to the left, even though it seems like it should be the other way around.\n\nWith horizontal translations of the reciprocal function, the horizontal asymptote doesn't change, but the vertical asymptote is translated by the same number of units." 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186180671 1064 "Because we know how to plot the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x},$ we can also plot horizontal translations of this graph. In general, the graph of \n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x-h}\n$$\n\nis a horizontal translation of the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ by $h$ units *right*.\n\nFor example, to plot the graph of \n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x {\\color{blue} \\, - \\, 1}},\n$$\n\nwe translate the original curve horizontally by $\\color{blue}1$ unit *right*:\n\n![](images/d4/d4778ef277b11ab33eb01a650ad07c4fd2e3902f303dd2fb15ed81a4b54649a0.png)\n\nThe graph of \n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x+h}\n$$\n\nis a horizontal translation of the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ by $h$ units *left*.\n\nFor example, to plot the graph of \n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x{\\color{red} \\, + \\, 3}},\n$$\n\nwe translate the original curve by $\\color{red}3$ units *left*:\n\n![](images/87/87bbb7c00e10db814c9fc67786940cf5891d96496a2e85ed7ee2023bb1af8e3d.png)\n\n**Watch out!** Horizontal translations are always counter-intuitive. The translation $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-h}$ is to the right and the translation $y=\\dfrac{1}{x+h}$ is to the left, even though it seems like it should be the other way around.\n\nWith horizontal translations of the reciprocal function, the horizontal asymptote doesn't change, but the vertical asymptote is translated by the same number of units." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186180672 1064 "We can combine horizontal and vertical translations of reciprocal functions.\n\nIn general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{x-h} + k\n$$\n\nis obtained from the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nby translating it\n\n- horizontally by $h$ units to the right, and\n- vertically by $k$ units up.\n\nThe same is true regarding translations of the negative reciprocal function. In general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{1}{x-h} + k\n$$\n\nis obtained from the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nby translating it\n\n- horizontally by $h$ units to the right, and\n- vertically by $k$ units up." 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186180672 1064 "We can combine horizontal and vertical translations of reciprocal functions.\n\nIn general, the graph of \n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{x-h} + k\n$$\n\nis obtained from the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ by translating it\n\n- horizontally by $h$ units to the right, and\n\n- vertically by $k$ units up.\n\nThe same is true regarding translations of the negative reciprocal function. In general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{1}{x-h} + k\n$$\n\nis obtained from the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}$ by translating it\n\n- horizontally by $h$ units to the right, and\n\n- vertically by $k$ units up." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186180673 1064 "We also know the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nso we can plot vertical translations of this function as well.\n\nFor example, to plot the function\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{x} \\, {\\color{blue} \\, +\\, 1.5}\n$$\n\nwe translate the original curve *up* by $\\color{blue}1.5$ units:\n\n![](images/01/0189d3fe1630049f5b4c7b1c13b134cc77454008e51450db5bf8d85b2050eeb8.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186180673 1064 "We also know the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x},$ so we can plot vertical translations of this function as well.\n\nFor example, to plot the function \n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{x} \\, {\\color{blue} \\, +\\, 1.5},\n$$\n\nwe translate the original curve *up* by $\\color{blue}1.5$ units:\n\n![](images/01/0189d3fe1630049f5b4c7b1c13b134cc77454008e51450db5bf8d85b2050eeb8.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186180753 1064 "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/c0/c0bd305d326688f49ecf0579ee42217a58a7521be8a410f619cbe26f5c443991.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the domain of $f(x)$ is $[1,2]$, and\n- the range of $f(x)$ is $[2,6]$.\n\nRecall that each point $(a,b)$ on the graph of\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}y=f(x)}\n$$\n\nhas a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on the graph of the inverse function\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}\n$$\n\nThat is to say, the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap.\n\n![](images/1d/1dd83a834e14f8f3679db1c013f23cce80ec9b2546393cf77ee613140c6b7b5f.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the domain of $f^{-1}(x)$ is $[2,6]$, and\n- the range of $f^{-1}(x)$ is $[1,2]$.\n\nIn general:\n\n- The domain of a function ${\\color{blue}f(x)}$ is the range of its inverse function ${\\color{red}f^{-1}(x)}$.\n- The range of a function ${\\color{blue}f(x)}$ is the domain of its inverse function ${\\color{red}f^{-1}(x)}$." 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186180753 1064 "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/c0/c0bd305d326688f49ecf0579ee42217a58a7521be8a410f619cbe26f5c443991.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n- the domain of $f(x)$ is $[1,2],$ and\n\n- the range of $f(x)$ is $[2,6].$\n\nRecall that each point $(a,b)$ on the graph of ${\\color{blue}y=f(x)}$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on the graph of the inverse function ${\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}.$ That is to say, the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap.\n\n![](images/1d/1dd83a834e14f8f3679db1c013f23cce80ec9b2546393cf77ee613140c6b7b5f.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the domain of $f^{-1}(x)$ is $[2,6],$ and\n\n- the range of $f^{-1}(x)$ is $[1,2].$\n\nIn general:\n\n- The domain of a function ${\\color{blue}f(x)}$ is the range of its inverse function ${\\color{red}f^{-1}(x)}.$\n\n- The range of a function ${\\color{blue}f(x)}$ is the domain of its inverse function ${\\color{red}f^{-1}(x)}.$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186198272 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561008 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186198272 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561022 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186198779 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times N\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/34/34f779801b1bb52d5834ab4e04c91d92585af9876f5ea687ad59cb6931e0144c.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/38/387709ae58ce7716338785f82e9159ce8570b046518554edd820c3ed31a23c53.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $N\\times N$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/14/141d0b84041957cf8415db4673c999f5213ff23553e57ab7e0e9120e648f306c.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186198779 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times N\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/34/34f779801b1bb52d5834ab4e04c91d92585af9876f5ea687ad59cb6931e0144c.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/38/387709ae58ce7716338785f82e9159ce8570b046518554edd820c3ed31a23c53.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $N\\times N$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/14/141d0b84041957cf8415db4673c999f5213ff23553e57ab7e0e9120e648f306c.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186198802 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=\\{0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-1,1\\}$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186198802 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={0,1,2}\\times {-1,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186198802 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing $\\{0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-1,1\\}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS=\\{(0,-1),(0,1),(1,-1),(1,1),(2,-1),(2,1)\\}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5fdae9b803df3873ee7deef4a36678e0d8274dd256fe331360261f1fe6dd492.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186198802 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing ${0,1,2}\\times {-1,1}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS={(0,-1),(0,1),(1,-1),(1,1),(2,-1),(2,1)}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5fdae9b803df3873ee7deef4a36678e0d8274dd256fe331360261f1fe6dd492.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186221410 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561040 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186253117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186250580 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186253117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186250934 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186253117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251433 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186253117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251466 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186253117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251874 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186253117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560363 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186253117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560388 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186253117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560603 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186253117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560707 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186253117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560744 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186253117 1078 #edb/ref 1759218