36042 17592186045417 false] [17592186281515 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+({t}^{3}+1)\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\bbox[5pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186284731 1071 "Let $I=[1,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times \\{i\\}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186284731 1071 "Let $I=[1,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times {i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186284731 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[0,2-1]\\times \\{1\\}=[0,1]\\times \\{1\\}=\\{(k,1)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddb7039dfcdbc2ef4070ce732c635a48194b6675cc3c78de6267e656c5e8387a.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times \\{i\\}=\\{(k,i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,2-i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,i)$ to the point $(2-i,i).$ \n\n![](images/e8/e83644a2f0de96ee58ea893028bb7a42681ee71a15d194f2b344c4c15f60fafe.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186284731 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[0,2-1]\\times {1}=[0,1]\\times {1}={(k,1)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddb7039dfcdbc2ef4070ce732c635a48194b6675cc3c78de6267e656c5e8387a.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times {i}={(k,i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,2-i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,i)$ to the point $(2-i,i).$ \n\n![](images/e8/e83644a2f0de96ee58ea893028bb7a42681ee71a15d194f2b344c4c15f60fafe.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set." 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186284732 1071 "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186284732 1071 "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186284732 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=\\frac{1}{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1/2}=\\{\\frac{1}{2}\\}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]=\\{\\frac{1}{2}\\}\\times [0,1]=\\{(\\frac{1}{2},k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/2827c5b33e84aff775d8bcc7ca98492afb220dda3a52fec4166afaa4da187d66.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)]=\\{(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi k))\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)).$ \n\n![](images/5a/5a108a3c0cdf37a072d135674b6ffdb3894b8c5ba142604ea7a434b0f42245f5.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set." 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186284732 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=\\frac{1}{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1/2}={\\frac{1}{2}}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]={\\frac{1}{2}}\\times [0,1]={(\\frac{1}{2},k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/2827c5b33e84aff775d8bcc7ca98492afb220dda3a52fec4166afaa4da187d66.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)]={(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi k))\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)).$ \n\n![](images/5a/5a108a3c0cdf37a072d135674b6ffdb3894b8c5ba142604ea7a434b0f42245f5.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set." 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186284750 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-1,1]\\times [-0.5,0.5]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-0.5,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-0.5),$ $(1,0.5),$ $(-1,0.5),$ and $(-1,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0a530434b8ac38fdb4cb517f22e121892c8b7c635c702c1cd9fccb2961aee18.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-i,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-i),$ $(1,i),$ $(-1,i),$ and $(-1,-i).$\n\n![](images/69/6982658bd8ce0e3c81953309ed9f2a70d1e56bbfce8af8862a32da66bf19e638.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[-1,1]\\times \\{0\\}:$\n\n![](images/34/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186284750 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-1,1]\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-0.5),$ $(1,0.5),$ $(-1,0.5),$ and $(-1,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0a530434b8ac38fdb4cb517f22e121892c8b7c635c702c1cd9fccb2961aee18.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-i),$ $(1,i),$ $(-1,i),$ and $(-1,-i).$\n\n![](images/69/6982658bd8ce0e3c81953309ed9f2a70d1e56bbfce8af8862a32da66bf19e638.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[-1,1]\\times {0}:$\n\n![](images/34/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186284751 1071 "Let $I=[-1,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-|i|,|i|].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186284751 1071 "Let $I=[-1,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [-|i|,|i|].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186284751 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{-0.5}=\\{-0.5\\}\\times [-0.5,0.5]=\\{(-0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5,-0.5)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7793d85e02ccd05c55c34971c90be087d58274eabd21f46fe09f08ccd60797b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-|i|,|i|]=\\{(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-|i|,|i|]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-|i|)$ to the point $(i,|i|).$ \n\n![](images/c3/c30712295adfe51c655662cc09f8f1e23b3ea97573693154e0e0b6ba48e15c07.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/8c/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186284751 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{-0.5}={-0.5}\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(-0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5,-0.5)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7793d85e02ccd05c55c34971c90be087d58274eabd21f46fe09f08ccd60797b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [-|i|,|i|]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-|i|,|i|]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-|i|)$ to the point $(i,|i|).$ \n\n![](images/c3/c30712295adfe51c655662cc09f8f1e23b3ea97573693154e0e0b6ba48e15c07.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/8c/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186287777 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times Z\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in Z\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/96/9673951b4e72ba503e740369462f9e528e43ffc083706ee558ade7ccd52702f5.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}=\\{(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/79/797b66a293eb241f841869abe97e324f4301907f71029acceefd68487bef1b4f.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup \\text{ }{S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times Z$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/de/de19fcf0f246b09d4bb957568e026b36de3f55c1a6c7065d79faacee794a8326.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186287777 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times Z\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/96/9673951b4e72ba503e740369462f9e528e43ffc083706ee558ade7ccd52702f5.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/79/797b66a293eb241f841869abe97e324f4301907f71029acceefd68487bef1b4f.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup \\text{ }{S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times Z$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/de/de19fcf0f246b09d4bb957568e026b36de3f55c1a6c7065d79faacee794a8326.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186287784 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative integer.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/37/37ad5627c3fb7cc89e751bb697e01ac7fecaf2712e230b6e3812ad9137d5cd46.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}=\\{(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/97/97ebcac85477b8e88564ab67d59c926d5faa72138c8a31d80d3b89895f33668c.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-5}\\cup {S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a7/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186287784 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative integer.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={