elongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing ${-2,2}\\times {-1,0,1}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS={(-2,-1),(-2,0),(-2,1),(2,-1),(2,0),(2,1)}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/5d/5dd3ff71d17188adaa68632b6d16aed1f41e1975ec4ddd321a8160fabb0e16b8.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186287873 1071 "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times \\{1-i\\}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186287873 1071 "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {1-i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186287873 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-0.5,0.5]\\times \\{0.5\\}=\\{(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/509d3e8d3bce0b422cad7e7f02377468cacc0de3a4e45647c12fef15f2c9991f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times \\{1-i\\}=\\{(k,1-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(-i,1-i)$ to the point $(i,1-i).$ \n\n![](images/21/21859c2ac02ba80d47672bf2fab25f7358feb921bc5b546c1e8c783c2819bea3.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/5b/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186287873 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-0.5,0.5]\\times {0.5}={(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/509d3e8d3bce0b422cad7e7f02377468cacc0de3a4e45647c12fef15f2c9991f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {1-i}={(k,1-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(-i,1-i)$ to the point $(i,1-i).$ \n\n![](images/21/21859c2ac02ba80d47672bf2fab25f7358feb921bc5b546c1e8c783c2819bea3.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/5b/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186287874 1071 "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-i,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186287874 1071 "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [-i,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186287874 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=\\{1\\}\\times [-1,1]=\\{(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-1,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f2/f26fa8a525806370f5505ad097ead2b289192bb3a6eb3e341eb7c2f91a03531f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-i,i]=\\{(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-i)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/bf/bfeafe4c3b800b33b4f8550de02c2eb908effb5c28ef59b40f8c5e2bcad38ac4.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/4d/4d860b60ab9e139b26c9f6e1d330188d5e752a51624a2374ad713b6974e66608.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186287874 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}={1}\\times [-1,1]={(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-1,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f2/f26fa8a525806370f5505ad097ead2b289192bb3a6eb3e341eb7c2f91a03531f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [-i,i]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-i)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/bf/bfeafe4c3b800b33b4f8550de02c2eb908effb5c28ef59b40f8c5e2bcad38ac4.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/4d/4d860b60ab9e139b26c9f6e1d330188d5e752a51624a2374ad713b6974e66608.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186287877 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,1]\\times [0,0.5]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,1],b\\in [0,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ $(0.5,1),$ and $(0.5,0)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0f/0fef44c4b8c4911a892d7dad4502edbb80ef236553d6fddb57de1472bd44a62b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,1]\\times [0,i]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,1],b\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ $(i,1),$ and $(i,0).$\n\n![](images/f4/f4602a46d3e4fc1799a475f05548ef278fdd33d354d1ec19ca4e5c5cc34cfcf8.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segments $[0,1]\\times \\{0\\}:$\n\n![](images/19/19a2cd951a55508269a0034aec582345bf7e350ec516fe1eecac0d6bf5847981.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 true] [17592186287877 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,1]\\times [0,0.5]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,1],b\\in [0,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ $(0.5,1),$ and $(0.5,0)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0f/0fef44c4b8c4911a892d7dad4502edbb80ef236553d6fddb57de1472bd44a62b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,1]\\times [0,i]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,1],b\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ $(i,1),$ and $(i,0).$\n\n![](images/f4/f4602a46d3e4fc1799a475f05548ef278fdd33d354d1ec19ca4e5c5cc34cfcf8.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segments $[0,1]\\times {0}:$\n\n![](images/19/19a2cd951a55508269a0034aec582345bf7e350ec516fe1eecac0d6bf5847981.png)" 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186350710 1075 #edb/ref 17592186348910 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186350710 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349923 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186350710 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350213 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [17592186350710 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350606 13194139536042 17592186045417 false] [